<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Nsamankow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nsamankow · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-ashanti-wars">Anglo-Ashanti Wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Nsamankow was fought on 22 January 1824 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, where an army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> annihilated a British expeditionary force under Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maccarthy">Sir Charles MacCarthy</a>, who was killed. It was one of the most significant defeats suffered by British forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. The battle forced a temporary British withdrawal and remained a symbol of Asante resistance to colonial expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-nsamankow.jpg" alt="Battle of Nsamankow" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C. Williams · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashantee_soldier2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
